## 2. Executive summary

- **Problem** `[H, strong analogous evidence]`: during busy periods clinicians lack quick, consolidated visibility of important patient information — appointments, basic history, reports and pending actions — forcing time-consuming navigation across HMIS screens, scanned documents and external records.
- **Target** `[V-reasoned]`: mid-size private multispecialty clinics (50–500 OPD/day) already running an HMIS; primary user the OPD consultant; economic buyer the clinic owner/medical director.
- **Evidence strength:** problem CLASS is STRONG (peer-reviewed time-motion literature [1][2][3]); demand for THIS product in Indian OPD is UNPROVEN. Infrastructure is verified ([8]).
- **Opportunity & position:** a read-only, source-attributed summary layered on the existing HMIS, conforming to HL7 International Patient Summary [7], with data-access failures made explicit — the trusted "first 30 seconds" view that complements, not replaces, the HMIS. Unmet need: no deployed system offers a single, visit-focused, cross-source, attributed view.
- **Major risks:** HMIS vendor builds it natively; clinician adoption; HMIS API access; device classification; willingness to pay.
- **Recommendation — GO to validate:** fund an 8-week validation gate (§11). Do not build the MVP until its Proceed criteria are met. This is a decision to validate, not yet to build.

## 3. Business context & strategic opportunity

India has the digital substrate for record exchange at national scale: ~93.95 crore ABHA IDs and 104+ crore linked records, July 2026 `[8, V-S]`. Yet clinical workflows still require assembling encounter history across multiple screens, scans and consented external records. Peer-reviewed data show ambulatory physicians spend ~49% of the clinic day on EHR/desk work vs ~27% on direct care [1], and ~16 min of EHR time per encounter, chart review a leading component [3]. If even a fraction of that cost transfers to Indian OPD, compressing it has clear operational value. We do **not** assume time pressure is uniform — low-volume/single-specialty clinics may see little benefit; a segmentation question tested in §9.

## 4. Problem definition & evidence

| Dimension | What we can say today | Grade |
|---|---|---|
| When/where friction occurs | Hypothesised at start of busy OPD encounters when assembling history; to be located precisely by contextual inquiry. | `[H]` |
| Most important information | Working set: active problems, allergies, current meds, recent results, last encounter, today's appointment context. Validate ranking with clinicians. | `[H]` |
| Frequency & severity | Analogous literature implies high frequency; India magnitude unmeasured. | `[H]/[Q]` |
| Current workarounds & cost | Manual search, paper, phone calls, asking the patient — slow, unverifiable, incomplete. | `[H]` |
| Root cause (critical) | Could be interface, fragmentation, data quality, access rules, or training. A dashboard only fixes interface+fragmentation. Interviews must isolate which dominates. | `[Q]` |

**Refined problem statement:** *"During high-volume periods, OPD consultants in digitised private multispecialty clinics cannot assemble a trusted, encounter-relevant picture of a patient quickly, because the needed information is scattered, inconsistently attributed, and sometimes inaccessible — costing consultation time and risking decisions made on incomplete information."*

**Counter-evidence / unresolved:** if the dominant root cause is data quality or access policy rather than presentation, the dashboard is the wrong primary intervention — captured in the Stop criteria.

## 5. "Pending access" — four distinct states (never interchangeable)

| State | Meaning | Product behaviour |
|---|---|---|
| Access requested | Access asked for, awaiting approval | Show "request pending"; do not imply absence |
| Consent pending | DPDP/ABDM consent not yet granted | Show "consent required/pending"; item hidden until valid consent |
| Access restricted | User's role not permitted | Show "restricted for your role"; log the denial |
| Retrieval failed | Data exists but fetch failed | Show "failed to retrieve"; **NEVER** render as "none" (allergy safety rule) |

`[Q]` Which of these states clinicians actually encounter, and in what proportion, is an open question for discovery.

## 6. Market & customer segmentation

| Segment | Workflow pain / data maturity | Integration & procurement | Verdict |
|---|---|---|---|
| Private multispecialty clinic, 50–500 OPD/day, HMIS in place | High OPD volume; digitised data; speed is material | Moderate integration; faster procurement than chains | **PRIMARY ICP** `[H]` |
| Small single-specialty clinic | Lower volume; thinner records; less pain | Simple but low willingness to pay | Deferred `[H]` |
| Hospital / teaching hospital | Rich data; real pain | Complex integration; long procurement; many stakeholders | Phase 2 `[H]` |
| Government facility (e-Hospital/ABDM) | Pain exists; policy-driven | Tender procurement; different terms | Not assessed `[Q]` |

**Recommended initial ICP & why:** mid-size private multispecialty clinics — they combine existing digitised data (so a summary has material to show), OPD volume high enough that seconds saved compound, and ownership-led procurement short enough to run a paid pilot inside two quarters.

## 7. Stakeholder & user analysis

| Stakeholder | Role in decision | Primary need | Conflict |
|---|---|---|---|
| OPD consultant | Primary user | Fast, trustworthy, complete view | Wants breadth & speed |
| Nurse / front desk | Secondary user | Scheduling & prep context | Narrower data scope |
| Clinic owner / medical director | Economic buyer | Throughput, ROI, low disruption | Cost vs benefit |
| CIO / IT admin | Influencer / implementer | Integration simplicity, security | Low maintenance burden |
| Privacy / security officer | Approver / veto | Minimum-necessary access, DPDP | Opposes broad access |
| Patient | Affected party | Consent, privacy | Controls external records |

**Key tension & resolution:** clinician breadth vs privacy-officer minimum-necessary — resolved by role-based, per-panel access with every view and denial logged.

## 8. Jobs to be done

- **JTBD-1:** grasp what matters about a patient in seconds on opening them (trigger: patient opened; barrier: scattered data).
- **JTBD-2:** be told clearly when data is missing or blocked, to avoid mistaking non-display for absence of a condition.
- **JTBD-3:** jump to the underlying record to verify without re-searching (trust + traceability).

| Need | Importance | Frequency | Evidence confidence |
|---|---|---|---|
| See active problems/allergies/meds at a glance | High | Every encounter | Medium (analogous) |
| Know data provenance & recency | High | Every encounter | Medium |
| Be warned of missing/blocked data | Critical (safety) | Intermittent | Medium |
| Appointment/scheduling context | Medium | Daily | Low — validate |

## 9. Voice-of-customer & validation plan (proposed — not yet conducted)

No primary research has been conducted. The following is the plan, not results.

- **Sample & recruitment:** 12–15 OPD consultants + 4–6 administrators across 5–6 private multispecialty clinics; purposive sampling across specialties and clinic sizes; theme-saturation basis. Optional confirmatory survey n≈97 for ±10 pts at 95% confidence (worst case p=0.5).
- **Method:** contextual inquiry + time-motion on ≥100 OPD encounters; friction scoring; buyer interviews with working prototype.
- **Bias controls:** neutral, non-leading questions; observe before asking; separate the person who benefits (clinician) from the person who pays (owner); don't demo before eliciting the problem.
- **Interview guide (neutral):**
  - Which patient details do you look for most when a patient walks in during a busy session?
  - Walk me through the last time finding patient information slowed you down. What did you do?
  - How do you handle a record that's incomplete or you can't access?
  - What does "pending access" or "waiting on records" mean in your actual workflow?
  - What would make a summary genuinely useful without adding to your mental load?
  - What would stop you adopting another screen/tool? (owner) What would justify paying for it?
- **Evidence thresholds:** confirm if ≥60% report weekly retrieval friction AND measured time-to-info is materially high; revise if 40–60%; reject if <40%.

## 10. Competitive landscape & substitutes

*(Feature claims from official documentation only; absence of documentation ≠ absence of feature.)*

| Alternative | Documented strength | Gap vs this concept | Evidence |
|---|---|---|---|
| Native HMIS summary (Bahmni [9], commercial suites) | In-workflow; no extra cost; configurable dashboard | Facility-scoped; chart by-product, not visit-specific cross-source attributed view | `[V-S]/[H]` |
| NIC e-Hospital [10] | Scaled public HMIS; ABDM-linked | Operations-oriented; no attributed encounter summary documented | `[V-S]/[H]` |
| ABDM patient-initiated pull [5] | Cross-facility consent framework exists | Per-request friction; clinician-side adoption unproven | `[V-P]/[H]` |
| Improve existing EHR (do nothing new) | Lowest cost; no new vendor | Doesn't deliver cross-source attributed view; depends on vendor roadmap | `[H]/[Q]` |
| Manual / paper / phone | Full user control | Slow, unverifiable, incomplete | `[H]` |

**Differentiation** `[H]`: one persistent, encounter-focused, IPS-conformant, source-attributed view across in-house + consented external records, with access failures explicit.
**Primary threat** `[H]`: HMIS vendor adds it natively at ~zero marginal customer cost → explicit PIVOT to vendor partnership. We do not infer competitor gaps merely from documentation silence.

